WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.694
Welcome to Assembly.

00:00:01.694 --> 00:00:07.202
This animation uses fictional delegations to explain negotiation.

00:00:07.202 --> 00:00:13.218
It draws on the published Global Model WHO twenty twenty-five handbook.

00:00:13.218 --> 00:00:19.742
The twenty twenty-six Geneva event is scheduled for November ten to thirteen.

00:00:19.742 --> 00:00:26.266
This is a teaching reconstruction, not footage or a prediction of that event.

00:00:26.266 --> 00:00:31.748
The opening plenary establishes the agenda and organisation of work.

00:00:31.748 --> 00:00:35.214
Committee work includes an expert briefing.

00:00:35.214 --> 00:00:42.470
Delegates can question the evidence before examining a starting text, called a zero draft.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:52.466
In our fictional example, the question is how digital infrastructure can develop while protecting water and health services.

00:00:52.466 --> 00:00:54.932
Data centres use electricity.

00:00:54.932 --> 00:01:02.840
Some cooling systems consume water, and electricity generation can add indirect water demand.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.091
The footprint varies with location and technology.

00:01:07.091 --> 00:01:11.513
Our exercise asks delegates to negotiate safeguards.

00:01:11.513 --> 00:01:20.866
It does not assume that every data centre has the same impact, or that this is an official Geneva agenda item.

00:01:20.866 --> 00:01:28.727
Aridia proposes: add protection for drinking water and essential health services during drought.

00:01:28.727 --> 00:01:37.489
Estara responds: we support safeguards, but cannot accept an undefined restriction on all digital services.

00:01:37.489 --> 00:01:46.333
Delegates identify the exact paragraph and explain both the requested change and its practical consequences.

00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:50.450
Unresolved wording can be bracketed and revisited.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.803
The facilitator asks which condition could resolve the objection.

00:01:55.803 --> 00:02:00.498
In this example, Aridia needs a credible drought trigger.

00:02:00.498 --> 00:02:03.956
Estara needs a predictable review process.

00:02:03.956 --> 00:02:10.133
The discussion shifts from opposing slogans to language both can implement.

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:23.535
Kitala proposes a package: publish site-level water information, protect essential services during verified shortages, and phase in reporting with technical support.

00:02:23.535 --> 00:02:26.703
These are fictional teaching proposals.

00:02:26.703 --> 00:02:30.764
Delegates test the wording against their mandates.

00:02:30.764 --> 00:02:38.400
A paragraph agreed provisionally is marked agreed ad referendum, pending the complete outcome.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.767
The committee reviews the negotiated text and seeks adoption without a vote.

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:48.118
Delegations may explain their positions.

00:02:48.118 --> 00:02:53.229
The committee outcome proceeds to plenary for final approval.

00:02:53.229 --> 00:02:58.004
Consensus does not mean everyone prefers every paragraph.

00:02:58.004 --> 00:03:06.466
An unresolved objection requires further work; an expiring classroom timer does not create agreement.

00:03:06.466 --> 00:03:09.931
Now apply the learning in your classroom.

00:03:09.931 --> 00:03:14.411
Record agreed wording and any unresolved differences.

00:03:14.411 --> 00:03:21.595
Assign implementation responsibilities, resources, a timetable and review indicators.

00:03:21.595 --> 00:03:32.328
Our teaching schedule provides ten minutes of prebrief, twenty of negotiation, fifteen for the report, and fifteen for debrief.

00:03:32.328 --> 00:03:37.400
These timings belong to Assembly, not the Geneva conference.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:44.869
The real Intergovernmental Negotiating Body worked over several years on a pandemic instrument.

00:03:44.869 --> 00:03:51.473
Its process concluded with the Pandemic Agreement adopted in May twenty twenty-five.

00:03:51.473 --> 00:03:54.854
It is distinct from the student simulation.

00:03:54.854 --> 00:04:01.537
Explore the linked WHO sources, then enter solo practice or join your live classroom.

00:04:01.537 --> 00:04:06.333
The goal is reasoned compromise, not simply winning a debate.
